{"uid":"a9a1e66b4ab77b4a","name":"test_a_refused_repoint_reaches_the_next_worker_prompt","fullName":"tests.test_worker_split_proposal#test_a_refused_repoint_reaches_the_next_worker_prompt","historyId":"c221afc2d941a0c32c69be0483a174d3","time":{"start":1791891290839,"stop":1791891290849,"duration":10},"description":"The reused feedback path, asserted so the reuse is deliberate rather\n    than incidental: a refused re-point lands under the SAME\n    `REFUSED_WORKER_SPLIT_STATUS_KEY` a refused split uses, and\n    `_worker_assignment_extra` renders its errors verbatim under the same\n    heading. No second status key and no second heading -- both are refused\n    optional plan-shaping proposals from one channel with no re-ask ladder,\n    and the errors already self-identify through their\n    `proposed_task_updates:` prefix.\n\n    The batch here is two entries, the first of them perfectly legal, so it\n    also pins the refuse-WHOLE property on the LINT branch (the budget\n    branch's own is pinned above): one bad entry leaves the good one's graph\n    exactly as it stood.\n\n    Two properties of the reuse itself, both from the unit 6 review. The\n    rendered BODY names the re-point channel: the heading is shared and\n    stays byte-identical (pinned four times in\n    `tests/test_worker_split_refusal_reprompt.py`), but a worker that sent no\n    split must not read that its split was refused and be told to fix one.\n    And a reply refused on BOTH channels keeps both refusals -- the key holds\n    one unread refusal, so the same-task write EXTENDS it instead of\n    replacing, since otherwise the second channel's refusal would silently\n    cost the first one its only trip back to the worker.","descriptionHtml":"<p>The reused feedback path, asserted so the reuse is deliberate rather<br />\nthan incidental: a refused re-point lands under the SAME<br />\n<code>REFUSED_WORKER_SPLIT_STATUS_KEY</code> a refused split uses, and<br />\n<code>_worker_assignment_extra</code> renders its errors verbatim under the same<br />\nheading. No second status key and no second heading -- both are refused<br />\noptional plan-shaping proposals from one channel with no re-ask ladder,<br />\nand the errors already self-identify through their<br />\n<code>proposed_task_updates:</code> prefix.</p>\n<pre><code>The batch here is two entries, the first of them perfectly legal, so it\nalso pins the refuse-WHOLE property on the LINT branch (the budget\nbranch's own is pinned above): one bad entry leaves the good one's graph\nexactly as it stood.\n\nTwo properties of the reuse itself, both from the unit 6 review. The\nrendered BODY names the re-point channel: the heading is shared and\nstays byte-identical (pinned four times in\n`tests/test_worker_split_refusal_reprompt.py`), but a worker that sent no\nsplit must not read that its split was refused and be told to fix one.\nAnd a reply refused on BOTH channels keeps both refusals -- the key holds\none unread refusal, so the same-task write EXTENDS it instead of\nreplacing, since otherwise the second channel's refusal would silently\ncost the first one its only trip back to the worker.\n</code></pre>\n","status":"passed","flaky":false,"newFailed":false,"newBroken":false,"newPassed":false,"retriesCount":0,"retriesStatusChange":false,"beforeStages":[{"name":"event_loop_policy","time":{"start":1791891119438,"stop":1791891119438,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"tmp_path_factory","time":{"start":1791891119438,"stop":1791891119438,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"monkeypatch","time":{"start":1791891290824,"stop":1791891290824,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters